window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-27T05:45:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Printed: ECB non-policy meeting postponed the minimum reserve ratio decision (Handelsblatt: after December, possibly spring 2027); US core PCE 3.3% y/y (3.2% expected), Q3 GDP revised to 4.3%; German Q3 GDP revised to +0.1% q/q; INSEE confidence 82; USTR 15% Section 301 proposal on EU goods; STOXX 600 −1.06% to 626.03","why":"The liquidity step is deferred, the Fed leg is firm at 97.8% for 9 Dec, German hard data cooled and the trade risk is formal."},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"Printed: eurozone ministers nominated Hernández de Cos as ECB president from 7 Feb 2027, with Boone and Brunnermeier for the board; Parliament and Governing Council consultations next, formal appointment in December","why":"Settles the ECB’s 2027 leadership; investors read it as continuity"},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"Printed: ECB account showed a large number of members ready to hike in October (90% priced for 17 Dec); GfK −30.2 (−29.0 to −29.9 expected); M3 +3.6%; BoE FSR held the CCyB at 2% and proposed a 50 bp Tier 1 cut; Italy sold €8.5B of 6-month BOTs (results not confirmed); STOXX 600 +1.8% to 636.98 with US markets shut","why":"The account confirms Tightening; the rally put the index back above 635 but under 640"},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"US half session (stocks close 13:00 ET), first US trade since the 25 Nov close; Italian medium and long-term BTP auction; EC economic sentiment and final consumer confidence, November (ESI prior 96.9, flash confidence −16.9); Spanish poll blackout runs to 28 Nov","why":"The ESI against 97 is a regime input; the VIX reopening decides whether a 640 close can restore Neutral; the BTP auction tests demand with the spread at 107 bp"},{"date":"2026-11-28","event":"French Senate public vote on the first (revenue) part of the 2027 finance bill (may run into 29 Nov)","why":"Decides whether the Senate text reaches the CMP with a revenue side the Assemblée rejected on 4 Nov"},{"date":"2026-11-29","event":"Spanish general election: final 40dB poll PP 31.8%, PSOE 30.9%, Vox 17.3%, PP plus Vox 187 seats; poll.graphics average 186 with 68% of simulations at or above the 176 majority; RTVE average 189 at about 90%; polls close 20:00, provisional count by midnight. OPEC+ ministerial meetings the same day, expected to roll over Q1 2027 output targets","why":"A clear PP-Vox majority is priced; a hung result or a PSOE recovery moves the IBEX and Spanish spreads on 30 Nov; OPEC+ sets the oil input for the ECB"},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"German CPI flash, November; German retail sales, October (prior −2.0% m/m); first session after the Spanish election","why":"The German flash leads the euro area print by a day"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area flash HICP, November (Trading Economics calendar; earlier listed here for 2 Dec; Pantheon 4.1%); final manufacturing PMI; euro area unemployment for October follows the same week","why":"The last inflation print before the 16-17 Dec ECB meeting; unemployment is 6.5% after upward revisions"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area final manufacturing PMI, November (flash 52.9)","why":"Confirms or revises the two-month high"},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Euro area final composite and services PMIs, November; euro area unemployment, October (prior 6.5%); euro area producer prices, October (prior +11.1% y/y)","why":"Final confirmation of the November flash and the last labour print before the ECB meeting"},{"date":"2026-12-07","event":"German factory orders, October (prior +4.4% m/m, +3.3% y/y); German industrial production follows on 8 Dec","why":"The German swing economy after the September rebound"},{"date":"2026-12-09","event":"Fed decision (8-9 Dec; CME 97.8% priced for 25 bp after core PCE 3.3%, with a second hike about 63% priced for January)","why":"Sets the dollar leg of EUR/USD below 1.10 and the global curve the Bund follows"},{"date":"2026-12-11","event":"Lecornu’s follow-up meeting with the civil-service unions after the professional elections, with first pay measures promised by mid-December","why":"Decides whether the 17 Nov strike becomes a December movement during the finance bill"},{"date":"2026-12-14","event":"Euro area industrial production, October (prior +0.2% m/m, −1.5% y/y)","why":"Whether the September monthly gain holds against a −1.5% annual rate"},{"date":"2026-12-16","event":"UK CPI, November (prior 3.6%, core 2.8%, services 3.7%); UK labour market report","why":"The last UK inflation print before the 17 Dec BoE decision, with December about 80% priced"},{"date":"2026-12-17","event":"ECB decision and projections (16-17 Dec; futures 90% priced for 25 bp to 2.75% on 26 Nov, Kocher expects the inflation projections revised up; the account says rates are within the range of neutral estimates); BoE decision at 4.00%; EU leaders’ December summit is to give final approval to the de Cos nomination","why":"The December hike is the base case for both; the projections set the 2027 path and the second hike"},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"ECB wage tracker update; BoJ decision (79% priced for a hike)","why":"The tracker is the main evidence against a wage-price loop; the BoJ leg sets USD/JPY after the yen’s safe-haven bid"}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"The ECB held at 2.50% on 29 Oct and the account published 26 Nov shows a large number of members could already have supported a hike then, arguing it would simply frontload the necessary tightening, with inflation fixings peaking at 4.5% for January 2027 and above 2.5% until Q1 2028; futures price a 90% chance of 25 bp to 2.75% on 17 Dec (Investing.com), and Kocher expects the December projections revised up. Eurozone ministers nominated Pablo Hernández de Cos, 55, BIS general manager and former Bank of Spain governor, to succeed Lagarde when she leaves on 7 Feb 2027, with Boone and Brunnermeier for the board seats of Schnabel (leaving 3 Jan) and Lane (31 May); the formal appointment is due in December. The reserve-ratio decision is postponed past December. The BoE is at 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike; its 26 Nov Financial Stability Report held the CCyB at 2% and proposed cutting major banks’ Tier 1 requirements by about 50 bp of risk-weighted assets, and Breeden said households can absorb higher rates. The Fed is 97.8% priced for a hike on 9 Dec."},"cbName":"ECB stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov, back above the 635 trigger, every sector rose, miners, autos and defence more than 3%, the BTP spread tightened to 107 bp and the OAT-Bund spread to about 147 bp","b":"The VIX stands at 28.29 from 25 Nov with no US session, the index is 3 points under the 640 restore line, the Euro Stoxx 50 is still 1.9% under its October close and eToro called the move a technical response rather than restored confidence","read":"A broad one-day relief rally on a tariff cap and a succession announcement, with the US shut, does not meet the restore test. Under the framework the posture stays Risk-off until a STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25; a close back under 635 resets the count.","trigger":"A STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25 restores Neutral; a BTP spread above 150 bp, an OAT spread above 180 bp or iTraxx Crossover above 400 bp makes the Risk-off a credit one."},{"a":"The Ifo is 90.6, the highest since May 2023, Germany’s flash composite PMI is 55.0 and euro area new orders rose for a fifth month","b":"GfK consumer climate is −30.2 with economic expectations turning negative, Destatis revised German Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q with exports −1.0% and equipment investment −3.5%, France’s composite PMI is 46.2 and euro area industrial production is −1.5% y/y","read":"Business sentiment and household and hard data disagree. The framework reads leading data first, so the regime stays Slowdown rather than Contraction, but it does not reopen Expansion until the composite PMI holds above 52 and the hard data turn.","trigger":"A composite final PMI above 52 on 3 Dec with EC sentiment above 97 on 27 Nov reopens Expansion; a composite under 51 or a negative Q4 German print deepens the Slowdown."},{"a":"The ECB account says a large number of members could have hiked in October, inflation will go higher than thought with fixings peaking at 4.5% in January, a December hike is 90% priced and Brent settled $96.57 after the Hormuz mine strikes","b":"The Bund is 3.26%, 32 bp under its September high, the account itself says rates lie within the range of neutral estimates, GfK economic expectations turned negative and German Q3 growth was revised down to +0.1%","read":"The bond market is pricing a short cycle that ends near 3%, not a 2027 extension; the Council is betting on energy pass-through and wages. The 1 Dec flash HICP and the December projections under a Lagarde who leaves on 7 Feb settle which side de Cos inherits.","trigger":"A November flash HICP above 4.0% on 1 Dec or TTF back above €95 keeps 2027 hikes priced; a core print at or below 2.5% and TTF under €80 price them out."},{"a":"The de Cos nomination, the tariff cap and the deferred reserve ratio lifted Milan 2.4% with all 40 FTSE MIB members up, narrowed the BTP spread to 107 bp and the OAT-Bund spread to about 147 bp","b":"Commerzbank fell 2.6% on UniCredit’s demand to replace nine of ten shareholder board members, BAWAG 5.7% on a blocked deal, Fitch rates the Western European sovereign outlook deteriorating, the Senate still has to pass the budget’s revenue part on 28 Nov and Spain votes 29 Nov","read":"Sovereign spreads are calm under the stress lines and bank stress is idiosyncratic rather than systemic. The French vote and the Spanish result are the next political tests; a clear PP-Vox majority is priced at 186 to 189 seats.","trigger":"An OAT-Bund spread above 180 bp or a BTP spread above 150 bp at a close turns the Risk-off into a sovereign one; a Senate pass on 28 Nov and a clear Spanish majority keep spreads range-bound."},{"a":"TTF rose only 0.8% to €87.75 and is 15.6% below its October close, with Norwegian supply recovering and the exchange trading weather","b":"Brent settled $96.57 after two tankers hit mines near Khasab, the IRGC claims control of both sides of Hormuz, Ras Tanura loadings were reportedly halted, storage is under 68% against an 86% average and Qatari LNG remains disrupted","read":"Gas trades weather and Norway while oil trades the Gulf. For Europe the gas channel matters more for HICP and the regime; the oil channel matters more for the ECB’s projections and the euro, and both rose into the 1 Dec flash.","trigger":"TTF under €80 at a close removes the bad flag; above €95 or a confirmed halt to Ras Tanura exports puts both energy channels on bad."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"A broad relief rally; the posture stays Risk-off until a close above 640 with the VIX under 25.","src":"Reuters via Yahoo Finance and Longbridge; ad-hoc-news; aktiencheck; Trading Economics; Investing.com","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 +1.8% to 636.98, back above the 635 trigger and 3 points under the 640 restore line, as USTR’s 15% cap eased tariff fears and the de Cos nomination read as continuity: all sectors up, miners, autos and defence over 3%, tech rebounds (Soitec +7%, STMicro +4.8%, Infineon +3.6%, ASML +3.2%), Imperial Brands +13.3% on Altria bid interest, Babcock +6.6% on an £8.7B contract, Siemens Energy +4.8%; Commerzbank −2.6% on UniCredit’s EGM demand, BAWAG −5.7% on a blocked Permanent TSB deal. Euro Stoxx 50 +2.04% to 6,014.74, DAX +2.08% to 25,215.52 (39 of 40 up), CAC 40 +1.6%, FTSE MIB +2.4%, FTSE 100 +1.75% to 10,609.30. US markets closed for Thanksgiving; VIX 28.29 from 25 Nov.","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/world-indices/articles/european-stocks-end-higher-easing-174244569.html"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"Investors read the choice as policy continuity; the euro firmed and Milan and Madrid led the rally.","src":"Xinhua; Al Jazeera; Edge Consultancy (Council release); Reuters via Yahoo Finance; Newsquawk/FT","tag":"Policy","title":"Eurozone ministers nominate Pablo Hernández de Cos, 55, BIS general manager and former Bank of Spain governor, as ECB president from 7 Feb 2027 when Lagarde leaves early, after the Dutch dropped Klaas Knot; Laurence Boone (France) and Markus Brunnermeier (Germany) recommended for the seats of Schnabel (leaving 3 Jan 2027) and Lane (31 May 2027); Parliament and Governing Council consultations next, formal appointment in December. Costa announced the package on X; Macron called it a balanced team, Sánchez a Spain that leads in Europe.","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261127/4691f4568ae54368b112eea813ec7932/c.html"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"Confirms the Tightening call and keeps 2027 hikes in play.","src":"Reuters via WKZO; ActionForex (account text); Investing.com (90% pricing); Newsquawk","tag":"Policy","title":"ECB account of the 28–29 Oct meeting: a large number of members could have supported a hike then, which would simply frontload the necessary tightening; inflation is likely to go higher than earlier thought, with fixings peaking at 4.5% for January 2027 and above 2.5% until Q1 2028, oil and gas futures above the September adverse scenario, no material second-round effects yet but a need to pre-empt them; rates still lie within the range of neutral estimates. Futures price a 90% chance of 25 bp on 17 Dec (Investing.com); Reuters says most policymakers have since signalled a December hike and investors doubt it will be the last.","url":"https://wkzo.com/2026/11/26/ecb-already-had-large-support-for-rate-hike-in-oct-accounts-show/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"German consumers are not joining the Ifo upturn; price expectations are rising again.","src":"ActionForex; Investing.com; Trading Economics; Newsquawk","tag":"Data","title":"German GfK consumer climate −30.2 for December from a revised −30.9 (consensus −29.0 to −29.9), the highest since July: income expectations −13.9 from −15.7, willingness to buy −11.5 from −11.9, willingness to save 15.6 from 17.2, economic expectations −1.6 from 2.1 (first fall in seven months), price expectations 2.8 from 1.0; Bürkl: stagnating at a low level, does not bode well for Christmas. Euro area M3 +3.6% y/y in October (3.5% expected), loans to households 3.1%, to firms 4.5%. French PPI +8.2% y/y in October from 6.9%.","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/662156-germany-gfk-confidence-stays-depressed-at-30-2-as-inflation-fears-rise-and-economic-optimism-fades/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"Oil and gas both rose into the 1 Dec flash HICP; TTF stays above the €80 bad line.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com and Irish Times; Trading Economics; Newsquawk; Squawk","tag":"Energy","title":"Brent settles $96.57, +1.2%, a third gain and about 5% on the week, after Iran’s Revolutionary Guards said two tankers, one a supertanker with over 2 million barrels, hit sea mines north of Khasab crossing Hormuz, the IRGC claimed full control of both sides of the strait, the Houthis claimed the Ras Tanura refinery attack and a drone hit the Slavyansk refinery in Krasnodar; WTI $84.84 with no holiday settlement. OPEC+ meets 29 Nov, expected to roll over Q1 2027 targets. TTF +0.8% to €87.75 on colder December forecasts with storage under 68% against an 86% five-year average.","url":"https://au.investing.com/news/commodities-news/oil-rises-on-supply-concerns-as-iran-claims-mine-strikes-on-ships-4797270"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The cap removed the near-term tariff escalation risk for EU exporters, the trigger for the rally.","src":"Reuters via Korea Times; Investing.com; Newsquawk; Bank of England","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR said on 25 Nov it intends to cap the proposed Section 301 overcapacity tariffs at the rates agreed in trade deals: 15% including MFN duties for the EU, Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Switzerland, 20% for China rising to 40% if the truce unravels, not stacked on the 10% and 12.5% forced-labour duties or sectoral tariffs, with nothing taking effect before the 25 Jan hearing; Investing.com says February or March at the earliest. China’s MOFCOM is firmly opposed and reserves countermeasures. The BoE Financial Stability Report held the CCyB at 2% and proposed cutting major banks’ Tier 1 requirements by about 50 bp of RWA.","url":"https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261126/us-proposes-tariffs-over-excess-production-retains-15-cap-for-korea-japan-eu"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The sell-off broadened from chips to banks; the posture stays Risk-off with the restore line 14 points away.","src":"Reuters via Longbridge; ad-hoc-news; dpa-AFX via ariva; finanzen.at; Cappnotes","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −1.06% to 626.03, a third close under the 635 trigger and 0.4 points above the 20 Nov low: banks about −3% (UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%, Commerzbank −3.9%), tech down for the ninth day in ten (ASM International −7.8%, Infineon −6.5%, ASML −5.8%, STMicro −4.7%), Prosus −4.9%, Rockwool −15.6%; autos +1.5% on the EU hybrid-import cap (VW +4.6%, BMW +3.7%), Remy Cointreau +13.1%, Bureau Veritas +6.6%, Sanofi +4.9%. Euro Stoxx 50 −1.82% to 5,894.46 (May low), DAX −1.26% to 24,702.13, CAC 40 −0.8%, IBEX −2.0%, FTSE 100 −0.38% to 10,426.94. S&P 500 closed −0.25% at 7,182.78 after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq −0.62%, Dow +0.33%, VIX 28.29.","url":"https://longbridge.com/en/news/306829837"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The rate matches the existing ceiling, so the direct hit is limited, but the stacking question and the EU’s rejection reopen the trade risk into 2027.","src":"The Hill (Politico EU quotes); LaPresse; Al Jazeera/Reuters; netz-trends (deadlines); Cappnotes","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR Section 301 determinations on 14 partners: proposed 15% on EU goods net of MFN and existing duties, 20% on China, 15% on Japan; comments due 5 Jan 2027, hearing from 25 Jan. Commission spokesperson Olof Gill: the EU strongly rejects the premise that Europe is a source of structural overcapacity and expects the 15% all-in ceiling of the July 2025 joint statement to be respected; Bernd Lange calls the findings absurd. Beijing calls overcapacity a false issue.","url":"https://thehill.com/policy/international/6244220-eu-rejects-us-tariff-proposal/amp/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The liquidity drain is deferred but the December hike is firm and the projections will likely move up; bonds rallied on the equity sell-off, not on the ECB.","src":"Democrata/Handelsblatt; Newsquawk and Global Eye (Kocher); Reuters via Longbridge (LSEG); Trading Economics; ANSA","tag":"Policy","title":"ECB non-policy meeting postpones the decision on doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% until after the December meeting and possibly to spring 2027 (Handelsblatt): Wunsch calls it a tax, Schnabel is opposed, Simkus in favour. Kocher: the ECB is likely to revise its inflation outlook up in December, demand is not driving prices, second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027, market pricing is not completely unreasonable. LSEG prices 87% for a December hike. Bund 3.22%, a three-month low; BTP spread 111.9 bp.","url":"https://www.democrata.es/en/economy/the-ecb-delays-deciding-on-raising-the-minimum-bank-reserves/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"German hard data lag the 90.6 Ifo and 55.0 PMI, keeping the regime in Slowdown; the hot US print firms the Fed leg and the dollar.","src":"Destatis PD 409; Boursorama/Reuters (INSEE); Cappnotes (BEA, CME); netz-trends","tag":"Data","title":"Destatis revises German Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q from +0.2%: exports −1.0%, imports −1.4%, equipment investment −3.5%, construction −0.3%, private consumption +0.1%, manufacturing value added −0.3%; +1.1% y/y calendar-adjusted. INSEE French consumer confidence 82 in November from a revised 81 (82 expected). US October core PCE 3.3% y/y (3.2% expected, +0.3% m/m), headline 3.8%; Q3 GDP revised to 4.3% annualised from 3.4%; CME prices a 9 Dec Fed hike at 97.8%.","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2026/11/PD26_409_811.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Gas eased but stays above the €80 bad line while the oil risk premium returned through the Red Sea and Saudi infrastructure.","src":"Trading Economics; Tradingpedia/ICE; The Nation/Reuters; The Private Banker; PiQ (Fitch)","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF −4.73% to €87.33 on milder overnight forecasts and higher Norwegian flows after four gains, with storage below 69% and Gulf LNG still disrupted; the IRGC stopped a container ship near Hormuz on 24 Nov. Brent settled $94.61 (+3.5%) and WTI $83.68 after the Houthis claimed an attack on Saudi Arabia’s Ras Tanura refinery and Riyadh’s financial district; Saudi Arabia downed four drones over Ras Tanura, Jizan port was hit and a drone was destroyed over Jeddah. OPEC+ meets 29 Nov. Fitch (24 Nov) sets the 2027 outlook for Western European sovereign ratings at deteriorating.","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/news/world/40073934"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday’s rebound was short-covering; the posture stays Risk-off with the index 7 points below the 640 restore line.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −0.66% to 632.74, back under the 635 trigger, as the chip rally fades: Aixtron −16.3% (UBS sell), STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASML −5.6%; miners almost −5% (Aurubis −15%, Antofagasta −8.1%); Sanofi +9.8% on its $41B Insmed deal, health care +2%. Euro Stoxx 50 −1.16% to 6,003.76, DAX −1.31% to 25,005.51, CAC 40 −0.05% to 8,040.49, FTSE 100 −0.13% to 10,467.07, IBEX −0.86%, FTSE MIB −1.84%.","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/european-shares-end-lower-as-tech-rally-fades-sanofi-gains-ce7851dbdd89f025"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Backs Germany’s 55.0 flash PMI and the ECB hawks, but the manufacturing setback and narrow base keep the regime in Slowdown.","src":"ActionForex; Reuters via RTE; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Data","title":"Ifo business climate 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 (90.5 expected), the highest since May 2023; current conditions 89.4, expectations 91.9; services +2.4, manufacturing −1.2 from +0.9. Fuest: recovery proceeding but lacks breadth. US Conference Board confidence 103.3 from 91.9 (92.2 expected).","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Keeps 17 Dec at 2.75% as the base case for the ECB and a further BoE hike in play.","src":"Reuters via Global Banking & Finance; Newsquawk; TMGM","tag":"Policy","title":"Sleijpen: a December ECB hike is likely, the direction is clear, the path after is unclear; France’s finances must be fixed in Paris. Simkus: more tightening needed in 2027 if inflation persists. BoE’s Greene backs another rate rise; 50 bp would need clear evidence of energy costs feeding wages and prices.","url":"https://www.globalbankingandfinance.com/ecb-policy-direction-clear-december-uncertain-sleijpen/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"A new trade risk for German exporters just as the Ifo improves; no rate or date confirmed.","src":"finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Trade","title":"Reports that Washington is preparing higher tariffs on imports from the EU, China and other countries over state subsidies (dpa-AFX); Reuters says new tariffs on 16 trading partners could be announced. DHL −3.1%, Kühne+Nagel −3.6%. S&P 500 −0.50% to 7,201.09, Nasdaq −1.48%, Dow +0.85% to 50,018.70, Nvidia −5.86%.","url":"https://www.finanzen.net/nachricht/aktien/roundup-aktien-europa-schluss-eurostoxx-schwach-rueckschlag-fuer-techwerte-16084557"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Gas stays above the €80 bad line, keeping the inflation score near its low.","src":"Trading Economics; Newsquawk; ANSA","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF +1.69% to €91.25 on cold into early December, weak wind and storage below 69%. Iran’s army chief Hatami orders Hormuz kept closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met; Qatar’s PM says Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks. Bund 3.26%, BTP-Bund 109.4 bp, EUR/USD +0.53% to 1.0961. FT: US delays about 60 Patriot interceptors for Ukraine.","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"}],"headline":"Europe bounced: the STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov, back above the 635 trigger but 3 points under the 640 restore line, as USTR said the proposed Section 301 tariffs on EU goods would be capped at the 15% deal rate, eurozone ministers nominated Spain’s Pablo Hernández de Cos as the next ECB president and the ECB account showed a large number of members ready to hike in October; miners, autos and defence rose more than 3%, Imperial Brands 13.3% on Altria bid interest, while Commerzbank fell 2.6% on UniCredit’s board shake-up. The Euro Stoxx 50 rose 2.04% to 6,014.74, the DAX 2.08% to 25,215.52 and the FTSE 100 1.75% to 10,609.30 with Wall Street shut for Thanksgiving. The Bund rebounded to 3.26%, the BTP spread narrowed to 107 bp, EUR/USD rose to 1.0975, TTF edged up 0.8% to €87.75 and Brent settled $96.57 (+1.2%) after Iran said two tankers hit mines near Hormuz; German GfK rose to −30.2. The regime stays Slowdown, the posture Risk-off with the VIX at 28.29 and the ECB Tightening with a December hike 90% priced.","marketClose":"Thu 26 Nov 2026","notes":["Closes for 26 Nov: STOXX 600 636.98 (+1.8%) is the Reuters wire via Yahoo Finance and Longbridge, confirmed by Investing.com and Eurasia Business News; the +1.75% shown is computed on the 626.03 prior (Trading Economics rounds to 638, +1.9%). Euro Stoxx 50 6,014.74 (+120.28, +2.04%) is ad-hoc-news via onvista (Trading Economics text 6,025, +2.2%). DAX 25,215.52 (+513.39, +2.08%) is ad-hoc-news, aktiencheck/comdirect and netz-trends/dpa-AFX; diebewertung’s 25,284.46 was a late-session snapshot. FTSE 100 10,609.30 (+182.36, +1.75%) is the Trading Economics table (its GB100 CFD) and matches the Squawk cash close of +1.75%; the Trading Economics news item says 10,617 (+190, +1.82%) and Reuters +1.8%, so the close could be up to 8 points higher. CAC 40 +1.6% and FTSE MIB +2.4% are percentages only; no IBEX or SMI close was confirmed.","Bonds and FX for 26 Nov: Bund 3.26% is Trading Economics (3.2576, +3.9 bp, previous 3.22); ANSA gives 3.25% (+2.3 bp), the BTP 4.32% (−2.6 bp), the OAT 4.72% (−1.3 bp) and the closing spread 107 bp (open 109), so the OAT-Bund spread of about 147 bp is derived from ANSA’s yields. EUR/USD 1.0975 is the ECB reference rate for 26 Nov as read from the ECB USD page summary (25 Nov 1.0935); Newsquawk’s range was 1.0957 to 1.0989 and Trading Economics shows 1.0961 early on 27 Nov (−0.07%), implying a 1.0969 close. The gilt was not confirmed for 26 Nov.","TTF €87.75 is the Trading Economics 26 Nov value (+0.69, +0.80%; its previous of 87.06 differs from the 87.33 recorded for 25 Nov); no exchange settlement page was opened. Brent $96.57 (+1.96, +1.2%) is the Reuters settlement via Investing.com and Global Banking and Finance; WTI had no settlement and $84.84 is the 13:21 ET quote. Storage under 68% against 86% is Trading Economics; GIE 68.9% on 23 Nov against 89% is Oilprice.","Data: GfK −30.2 and the sub-indices are ActionForex and Trading Economics; the consensus differs by source (−29.0 ActionForex and FinScans, −29.4 RTT, −29.9 Trading Economics and Newsquawk) and the NIM release was not opened. M3, loans and French PPI are Newsquawk’s data table. The Italian BOT auction results and the 25 Nov BTP auction results were not found on any page read; QuiFinanza gives the €8.5B 6-month size.","Policy: the de Cos nomination is Xinhua, Al Jazeera and the Council release via Edge Consultancy; the 7 Feb 2027 departure, the Boone and Brunnermeier recommendations and Schnabel’s 3 Jan date are the same pages; the Dutch withdrawal of Knot is Newsquawk citing the FT. The account quotes are Reuters via WKZO and the ActionForex copy of the ECB text (the 4.5% fixings peak and the neutral-range line are verbatim; the $115 Brent and €97 gas assumptions are from a summary and not used in the rows). The 90% December probability is Investing.com’s futures line; aktiencheck says over 90%. The BoE capital figures are the FSR page (about 50 bp of RWA; Newsquawk said 60 bp). The USTR cap wording is Reuters via the Korea Times; the Commission’s reaction on 26 Nov was not found on a page read.","Score changes this run: markets +3 to 29 on the STOXX 600 back above 635, every sector up, the BTP spread at 107 bp and the OAT-Bund spread inside 150 bp, held back by the VIX at 28.29 and the index under 640; growth unchanged at 45 (GfK up but economic expectations negative); inflation unchanged at 29 (TTF and Brent both higher); policy unchanged at 27 (hike 90% priced, succession settled); composite 33 from 32. No score moved 5 or more."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Stays Risk-off, but only just. The STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov, back above the 635 trigger for the first time since 23 Nov, after USTR said the Section 301 tariffs would be capped at the 15% deal rate for the EU and eurozone ministers nominated Hernández de Cos as ECB president; every sector rose, miners, autos and defence more than 3%, Imperial Brands 13.3% on Altria bid interest, while Commerzbank fell 2.6% on UniCredit’s board shake-up and BAWAG 5.7%. The Euro Stoxx 50 rose 2.04% to 6,014.74, the DAX 2.08% to 25,215.52 (39 of 40 up) and the FTSE 100 1.75% to 10,609.30. The 640 restore line is 3 points away and the VIX stands at 28.29 from 25 Nov with no US session on 26 Nov, so neither restore condition holds at a close. Sovereigns are calm: the BTP-Bund spread closed 107 bp and the OAT-Bund spread about 147 bp, under the 150/180 bp lines. Neutral returns on a STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25."},"regime":{"label":"Slowdown","note":"Stays Slowdown. The only new data on 26 Nov were soft: German GfK consumer climate rose to −30.2 for December from a revised −30.9 (−29.0 to −29.9 expected), the highest since July but with economic expectations down to −1.6 from 2.1, the first fall after six gains, and price expectations up to 2.8 from 1.0; euro area M3 grew 3.6% y/y in October (3.5% expected) with loans to firms at 4.5%. Destatis revised German Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q on 25 Nov, so the 90.6 Ifo and the 55.0 German flash PMI still run ahead of the hard data. The euro area flash composite PMI is 51.7, between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion, with France at 46.2; September industrial production is −1.5% y/y, retail sales +0.4% y/y, unemployment 6.5%, Q3 GDP +0.4% q/q. EC sentiment on 27 Nov, flash HICP on 1 Dec and the final PMIs on 1 and 3 Dec are next."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":48.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":50.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.2},{"l":"May 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":52.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":52.9}],"latest":"52.9","name":"HCOB Eurozone Manufacturing PMI","next":"Early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"52.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash rose to 52.9 from 52.7, a two-month high and above the 52.8 Reuters poll, the tenth month above 50; the output index rose to 53.3 from 52.8. Total new orders across the economy rose for a fifth month at a slightly quicker pace and manufacturers’ order growth was stronger than services’, while new export orders grew at the slowest pace in three months; factory hiring was at a near three-and-a-half-year high. Input-cost inflation eased to a three-month low and selling prices rose at the weakest pace since August. Germany rose to 55.0 from 54.2 with output at 57.4, a 63-month high; France fell to 47.9 from 49.5 with output at 44.5, the weakest in 21 months. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, beat poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Two-month high","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"−0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":52.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":47.6},{"l":"May 26","v":47.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":49.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.2}],"latest":"51.2","name":"HCOB Eurozone Services PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.4 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash fell to 51.2 from 51.4, a five-month low and below the 51.8 Reuters poll that had looked for a rise. Services cut jobs for a second month, the year-ahead outlook fell for a fourth month to its lowest since May and output-price inflation slowed. Germany’s services PMI rose to 54.0 from 52.5; France’s rose to 46.6 from 46.2 but stayed in contraction with new business down markedly, and S&P Global’s Hayes said the French data raise the odds of a Q4 technical recession. The rest of the euro area grew at its slowest pace in six months; Finimize’s read of Williamson is that services feel high living costs and political uncertainty while investment orders hold up.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, missed poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Five-month low","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"0.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":48.8},{"l":"May 26","v":48.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":50},{"l":"Jul 26","v":52},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.7}],"latest":"51.7","name":"HCOB Eurozone Composite PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash composite held at 51.7, unchanged from October and below the 51.9 Reuters poll, a fifth straight month above 50; it sits between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion. Manufacturing (52.9) carried the index while services (51.2) slipped. New orders rose for a fifth month and slightly faster, employment was broadly unchanged, input costs rose at a three-month low pace and output prices at the weakest since August. Germany’s composite rose to 55.0 from 53.4, a 56-month high; France fell to 46.2 from 46.6, its sharpest contraction in six months with sentiment near its worst since 2012 outside Covid. Williamson: steady growth consistent with a near 0.3% q/q pace in Q4, masking worrying divergences.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, below 52","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics (23 Nov)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Unchanged","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"+1.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":-13.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-12.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-12.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-16.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-20.5},{"l":"May 26","v":-18.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-17.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-15.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-15.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-16.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":-17.9},{"l":"Nov 26","v":-16.9}],"latest":"−16.9","name":"Euro Area Consumer Confidence","next":"27 Nov (final, with ESI)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"−17.9","read":"The November flash rose to −16.9 from −17.9 in October, one point better than the −17.9 consensus and the first improvement since August, but still below the −15 weak line of the framework and about six points under the long-run average of −11. The final October economic sentiment indicator was 96.9, a four-month low; the November ESI and the final confidence figure print on 27 Nov. Consumers remain the weak leg of the leading set, with German business surveys holding up and the French INSEE climate at a June low.","ref":-11,"refLabel":"−11 = long-run average","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Weak, below −15","src":"European Commission flash (20 Nov) via Newsquawk and fx.co; Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"+7.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":45.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":59.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-0.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-17.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-10.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":26.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":34.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":34.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":28.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":35.6}],"latest":"35.6","name":"ZEW Economic Sentiment, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"28.1","read":"Rose to 35.6 in November from 28.1, above the 28.3 consensus (Newsquawk 29) and recovering most of October’s fall. Current conditions improved to −33.2 from −35.9, in line with consensus, and euro area expectations rose to 26.5 from 18.5 against 18.3, with the euro area current index at −14.7. Wambach: the German economy remains on its recovery track at a slow rate, supported by rising manufacturing orders and lower oil prices, but government spending has been the main driver so far, which raises debt-sustainability concerns. One beat does not change the regime; the 23 Nov flash PMIs and 24 Nov Ifo follow.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = neutral","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above consensus","src":"ZEW via ActionForex and Newsquawk (17 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661152-germany-zew-sentiment-jumps-to-35-6-but-recovery-remains-uneven/"},{"change":"+0.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":87.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":87.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":88.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":86.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":84.4},{"l":"May 26","v":84.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":85.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":86.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":88.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":89.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":90.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":90.6}],"latest":"90.6","name":"Ifo Business Climate, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec (December)","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"90.1 (Oct, rev. from 90.2)","read":"The Ifo rose to 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 against 90.5 expected (Reuters poll), a seventh straight rise and the highest since May 2023. Current conditions rose to 89.4 from 88.8 and expectations to 91.9 from 91.5 (ActionForex and Trading Economics; Newsquawk and QNC print 91.6), slightly under the 92.0 consensus; services rose to +2.4 from −1.2 while manufacturing fell back to −1.2 from +0.9, its first setback after six gains, with expectations negative again. Fuest said the recovery is proceeding but lacks breadth and is mostly driven by deficit-financed public spending; the reading backs the 55.0 German flash PMI but not a broad-based upturn.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since May 2023","src":"Ifo Institute via ActionForex (sector balances), Reuters via RTE (poll 90.5, Fuest), Trading Economics, Newsquawk","trend":"up","trendWord":"Seventh rise","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−0.4 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-1.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-1.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-1.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-1.5}],"latest":"−1.5%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"14 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"−1.1%","read":"Rose 0.2% on the month in September, the first monthly gain in four months, but fell 1.5% on the year from −1.1% in August (August −0.5% m/m and −1.1% y/y, unrevised). Durable consumer goods rose 1.3% m/m and intermediate goods 0.7%, capital goods were flat, energy fell 1.7% and non-durable consumer goods 0.4%; the EU rose 0.5% m/m and fell 0.8% y/y, with Ireland, Bulgaria and Romania the largest annual falls and the Netherlands, Hungary, Lithuania and Malta the largest rises. The annual rate is the weakest of the year and sits against a manufacturing PMI of 52.7, so the coincident set still does not confirm the survey; ING’s Colijn said signs of a true rebound are not there. The Eurostat page redirected, so the figures are the Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion reports of the release.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Fourth annual fall, m/m up","src":"Eurostat (4-16112026-AP, 16 Nov 2026) via Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-16112026-ap"},{"change":"−0.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1},{"l":"May 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.3% (rev. from +0.8%)","read":"Volumes fell 0.7% on the month in September against a −0.1% consensus, the sharpest fall in over a year, and rose 0.4% on the year after August was revised up to +0.4% m/m and +1.3% y/y. Non-food fell 1.2% m/m, food 0.3% and fuel rose 1.0%; Germany fell 2.0% m/m and 2.4% y/y, France 0.6% m/m (+2.8% y/y), Spain 0.4% and Italy rose 0.1%. Consumers are cutting real spending as HICP runs at 4.0% and confidence sits at −17.9; the annual rate is still positive, so the signal is warn rather than bad.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Sharp monthly fall","src":"Eurostat (6 Nov 2026 release)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/en/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-06112026-ap"},{"change":"0.0 pt (+0.1 pt vs first print)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.4},{"l":"May 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.5}],"latest":"6.5%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"6.5% (rev. from 6.4%)","read":"September unemployment printed 6.5% against 6.4% expected, and the Eurostat series now shows July and August at 6.5% too (revised from 6.4%), so the rate has risen 0.2 pt from the 6.3% low of January. 11.555M people are unemployed, up 122K on the month, youth unemployment rose to 15.4% from 15.1%; Spain 10.3%, France 8.5%, Italy 6.3%, Germany 4.1%, Netherlands 4.0%; the EU rate is 6.2%. The level is still low in the five-year range but the direction has turned.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Rising from 6.3% low","src":"Eurostat API (une_rt_m, updated 30 Oct); Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/unemployment-rate"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":10.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":13},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.6},{"l":"May 26","v":6.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":13.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.3}],"latest":"+3.3%","name":"German Factory Orders, y/y","next":"7 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+3.2% (rev. from +2.7%)","read":"Orders rose 4.4% on the month in September against a 4.8% consensus, recovering part of the August fall, which was revised to −10.1% from −10.6%. Orders are 3.3% above a year ago, and the August annual rate was revised to +3.2% from +2.7%. The rebound was broad: +5.1% excluding large orders, domestic +6.6%, foreign +2.8% (euro area +4.5%), autos +10.5% and machinery +8.3%, while other transport equipment fell 10.4%. Q3 orders were only 0.2% above Q2 and real manufacturing turnover rose 0.2% on the month, so the level is flat, not accelerating.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Broad rebound","src":"Destatis (press release 385, 5 Nov 2026); Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound m/m","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2026/11/PD26_385_421.html"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q4 23","v":0},{"l":"Q1 24","v":0.4},{"l":"Q2 24","v":0.2},{"l":"Q3 24","v":0.5},{"l":"Q4 24","v":0.3},{"l":"Q1 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Q2 25","v":0},{"l":"Q3 25","v":0.3},{"l":"Q4 25","v":0.2},{"l":"Q1 26","v":0},{"l":"Q2 26","v":0.6},{"l":"Q3 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Real GDP, q/q","next":"Early Dec (Q3 third estimate)","period":"Q3 2026 second estimate","prior":"+0.6%","read":"The second estimate on 13 Nov confirmed Q3 growth at +0.4% q/q (consensus 0.4%) and +1.3% y/y after 0.6% in Q2, with employment stable on the quarter: Ireland +5.5%, Spain +0.6%, Germany +0.2%, Italy 0.0%, France −0.1%; the EU grew 0.5%. Growth is positive, so the signal is good, but the composition is AI investment, defence and public spending with France and Italy flat to negative, the September goods balance swung to a €10.3B deficit on energy imports, and the ECB expects the yield rise to cost about 100 bp of growth over 2027-28. UK Q3 GDP rose 0.7% q/q the day before.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"good","signalWord":"Confirmed +0.4%","src":"Eurostat (2-13112026-AP, 13 Nov) via investingLive, Newsquawk and Eunews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, beat","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/2-13112026-ap"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4}],"latest":"4.0%","name":"HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"3.8%","read":"Eurostat confirmed October HICP at 4.0% y/y on 18 Nov, up from 3.8% and the highest since September 2023. Energy was 21.0% (19.0% in September; flash 20.8%), services 3.3% (3.2%), non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%) and food, alcohol and tobacco 1.5%, revised from 1.6% in the flash (1.3% in September). Lithuania printed 6.1%, Bulgaria 5.9%, Greece 5.7% and Italy 5.6%, up 1.4 pt on the month; Germany’s final was 2.9%. Pantheon projects 4.1% in November, 4.3% in December and a 4.5% peak in January, and the ECB expects inflation above 4% for much of the first half of 2027. The November flash is due 1 Dec. The Eurostat release page redirected to a maintenance notice; the figures are from Eunews and Trading Economics.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Confirmed, highest since Sep 2023","src":"Eurostat (2-18112026-AP, 18 Nov) via Eunews and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.eunews.it/en/2026/11/18/eurozone-inflation-confirmed-at-4-per-cent-in-october/"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.2},{"l":"May 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6}],"latest":"2.6%","name":"Core HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"2.5%","read":"Core HICP was revised up to 2.6% in the 18 Nov final from the 2.5% flash, a five-month high and the second monthly rise, with services at 3.3% (3.2% in September) and non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%). The energy shock is starting to show in underlying prices, though the ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov still has negotiated wages at 2.3% for 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027. Lagarde said underlying inflation would take a particular role in the December decision; a core print above 2.6% in the 1 Dec flash strengthens the case for the market’s second 2027 hike. The Q3 negotiated wages print is 20 Nov.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Five-month high","src":"Eurostat (18 Nov final) via Trading Economics; ECB wage tracker (4 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.5},{"l":"26 Nov","v":2.5}],"latest":"2.50%","name":"ECB Deposit Facility Rate","next":"16–17 Dec meeting (projection round)","period":"Since 16 Sep; held 29 Oct","prior":"2.25%","read":"The deposit rate is 2.50% after hikes in June and September and a hold on 29 Oct; a 25 bp hike to 2.75% on 17 Dec is the base case (LSEG 87% on 25 Nov per Reuters; Trading Economics cites two more hikes priced by March 2027). Kocher said on 25 Nov that the ECB is likely to revise its inflation outlook up in December, that demand is not driving prices, that second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027 and that market pricing is not completely unreasonable; Sleijpen called a December hike likely on 24 Nov with the path after unclear. The 25 Nov non-policy meeting postponed any decision on doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% until after the December meeting and possibly to spring 2027 (Handelsblatt via Democrata), with Wunsch calling it a tax, Schnabel opposed and Simkus in favour. The account of the October meeting is published 26 Nov and the European Council begins consultations on the presidency the same day.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening","src":"ECB key rates; Reuters via Longbridge (LSEG 87%); Newsquawk and Global Eye (Kocher); Democrata/Handelsblatt (reserve ratio postponed); Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/key_ecb_interest_rates/html/index.en.html"},{"change":"+0.12 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q3 25","v":1.89},{"l":"Q4 25","v":2.95},{"l":"Q1 26","v":2.56},{"l":"Q2 26","v":2.45},{"l":"Q3 26","v":2.57}],"latest":"2.57%","name":"Negotiated Wages, y/y","next":"Feb 2027 (Q4); tracker 18 Dec","period":"Q3 2026","prior":"2.45% (rev. from 2.44%)","read":"Negotiated wages rose 2.57% y/y in Q3 2026 from a revised 2.45% in Q2, a modest acceleration of 0.12 point and far below the 5.55% peak of 2024, so the 4.0% HICP print has not yet fed into pay settlements. The ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov shows 2.7% for the second half of 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027 with smoothed one-offs, on 45.6% and 31.8% coverage; the September staff projection has compensation per employee at 3.3% for 2026. This remains the main argument that the energy shock is not turning into a wage-price loop, and Nagel said on 19 Nov he sees no spiral. The next tracker update is 18 Dec, after the ECB meeting.","ref":3,"refLabel":"3% = roughly consistent with 2% inflation","signal":"good","signalWord":"No wage spiral","src":"ECB indicator of negotiated wages (20 Nov) via Poland Insight; ECB wage tracker release of 4 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/negotiated-wage-growth"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.8},{"l":"May 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.6}],"latest":"3.6%","name":"UK CPI, y/y","next":"16 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"3.5%","read":"Rose to 3.6% in October from 3.5%, a one-year high but below the 3.7% consensus and the BoE’s 3.7% projection. Core held at 2.8%, services eased to 3.7% (consensus 3.8%, BoE 3.9%), goods rose to 3.5% from 3.2% and CPIH to 3.7%; prices rose 0.5% on the month. Transport, mainly motor fuels, was the largest upward contribution with the earlier tobacco duty rise, restaurants and hotels the main offset; food rose 0.9%, the smallest since September 2021. The BoE projects nearly 5% in Q2 2027 on energy. December hike pricing fell to about 80% from 90% with three more hikes by June still priced; the 10-year gilt fell 9 bp to 5.30%. Next release 16 Dec.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoE target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"One-year high, below consensus","src":"ONS (18 Nov 2026 bulletin); Reuters via Investing.com; Trading Economics (gilts and pricing); Alliance News via BritBrief","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/bulletins/consumerpriceinflation/october2026"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.75},{"l":"May 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.75},{"l":"26 Nov","v":4}],"latest":"4.00%","name":"BoE Bank Rate","next":"17 Dec","period":"Since 5 Nov 2026","prior":"3.75%","read":"Bank Rate is 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike, the first since 2023, by 8 votes to 1. October CPI of 3.6% (3.7% expected) cut December pricing to about 80%, and the 20 Nov data were firm: retail sales rose 0.7% m/m (flat expected, ex-fuel +0.9%, +2.3% y/y), GfK confidence improved to −22 from −24 and October borrowing was £17.0B against £18.0B expected and a revised £22.9B in September. Trading Economics says markets price at least three more hikes by the end of 2027. The 10-year gilt fell below 5.3% to 5.24% on global risk aversion as the FTSE 100 lost 1.02% and 5.1% on the week; sterling ended about 1.31. The UK-EU Brussels summit agreed to link emissions trading systems, reduce agri-food checks from 2028 and start an economic-security dialogue. Greene told a SIGNUM roundtable on 24 Nov that she backs another rate increase, that disinflation has stalled and that a 50 bp move would need clear evidence of energy costs passing into wages and prices (TMGM summary).","signal":"warn","signalWord":"First hike since 2023","src":"Bank of England (Monetary Policy Summary, 5 Nov 2026); ONS retail sales and public finances via MT Newswires and Share Talk (20 Nov); Trading Economics gilt news (20 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+3.9 bp (TE 3.2576)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.81},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.91},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.05},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.96},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.07},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.58},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.32},{"l":"26 Nov","v":3.26}],"latest":"3.26%","name":"10-Year Bund Yield","period":"26 Nov","prior":"3.22% on 25 Nov","read":"The Bund rose to 3.26% on 26 Nov (Trading Economics 3.2576, +3.9 bp from 3.22; ANSA has 3.25%, +2.3 bp), giving back part of Wednesday’s rally as equities rebounded on the tariff cap, safe-haven demand eased and Brent rose to $96.57; Newsquawk had the Bund future −45 ticks. The ECB account said a large number of members could have hiked in October and that rates still lie within the range of neutral estimates, and futures price a 90% December hike, so the front end is anchored near 3% while the 10-year sits 32 bp under its September high of 3.58%. The gilt was about 5.14% on 25 Nov and the US 10-year 4.85% with no US session on 26 Nov.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Above 3%","src":"Trading Economics (3.2576, +3.9 bp, previous 3.22); ANSA (Bund 3.25%, +2.3 bp); Newsquawk (Bund future −45 ticks); Investing.com (90% December pricing)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/germany/government-bond-yield"},{"change":"−5 bp (opened 109 bp)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":74},{"l":"Jan 26","v":68},{"l":"Feb 26","v":64},{"l":"Mar 26","v":82},{"l":"Apr 26","v":82},{"l":"May 26","v":79},{"l":"Jun 26","v":77},{"l":"Jul 26","v":81},{"l":"Aug 26","v":81},{"l":"Sep 26","v":106},{"l":"Oct 26","v":118},{"l":"26 Nov","v":107}],"latest":"107 bp","name":"BTP–Bund 10-Year Spread","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"112 bp on 25 Nov","read":"The spread closed 107 bp on 26 Nov per ANSA, from 111.9 bp, after opening at 109 bp, with the BTP down 2.6 bp to 4.32% and the Bund up 2.3 bp to 3.25%; Milan led Europe with the FTSE MIB up 2.4% and all 40 members higher as bank fears from the reserve-ratio debate faded and the de Cos nomination read as continuity. It stays well under the 150 bp warn line. The OAT fell 1.3 bp to 4.72%, so the OAT-Bund spread is about 147 bp, back inside the 150 bp line ahead of the Senate’s 28 Nov revenue vote. Italy sold €8.5B of 6-month BOTs on 26 Nov (results not confirmed on a page read) and sells medium and long BTPs on 27 Nov; Spain votes 29 Nov.","ref":150,"refLabel":"150 bp = warn line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Under 150 bp","src":"ANSA (open 109 bp, close 107 bp, BTP 4.32%, Bund 3.25%, OAT 4.72%); QuiFinanza (€8.5B BOT auction); Investing.com and Eurasia Business News (FTSE MIB +2.4%)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Tighter","url":"https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/topnews/2026/11/26/lo-spread-tra-btp-e-bund-tedeschi-chiude-in-ribasso-a-107-punti_7f7dfd13-11ce-410e-91ef-8b229616a6cb.html"},{"change":"+1.75% on 26 Nov (Reuters 1.8%)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":592.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":611},{"l":"Feb 26","v":633.85},{"l":"Mar 26","v":583.14},{"l":"Apr 26","v":611.28},{"l":"May 26","v":626},{"l":"Jun 26","v":641.73},{"l":"Jul 26","v":649.19},{"l":"Aug 26","v":651.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":634.89},{"l":"Oct 26","v":646.75},{"l":"26 Nov","v":636.98}],"latest":"636.98","name":"STOXX Europe 600","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"626.03 on 25 Nov","read":"The STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov (Reuters via Yahoo Finance and Longbridge; +10.95 points on the 626.03 prior), its second gain in nine sessions and 4.1% below the 13 Nov record of 664.02, after USTR said the proposed Section 301 tariffs would be capped at the 15% EU deal rate and eurozone ministers nominated Hernández de Cos to lead the ECB. All sectors rose: miners, autos and defence more than 3%, technology after two days of selling (Soitec +7%, Infineon +3.6%, STMicro +4.8%), Imperial Brands +13.3% on Altria’s possible all-cash offer and Japan Tobacco interest, Babcock +6.6% on an £8.7B submarine-facilities contract, Siemens Energy +4.8% on a Venezuela alliance; Commerzbank fell 2.6% on UniCredit’s EGM demand and BAWAG 5.7% after the Irish High Court blocked its Permanent TSB deal, Equinor −0.5% on cut guidance. Reuters puts the index 7.6% up this year against 5% for the S&P 500. It is 2 points above the 635 trigger and 3 under the 640 restore line, with the VIX at 28.29 from 25 Nov, so the posture stays Risk-off.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Under 640 restore line","src":"Reuters via Yahoo Finance and Longbridge (636.98, +1.8%, sectors, movers); Investing.com (close, 90% ECB pricing); Trading Economics (638, +1.9%); Eurasia Business News (index moves)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Back above 635","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/world-indices/articles/european-stocks-end-higher-easing-174244569.html"},{"change":"+2.04% on 26 Nov (+120.28)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":5791.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":5947.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6138.41},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5569.73},{"l":"Apr 26","v":5881.51},{"l":"May 26","v":6050.54},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6328.09},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6358.01},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6420.16},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6269.02},{"l":"Oct 26","v":6131.31},{"l":"26 Nov","v":6014.74}],"latest":"6,014.74","name":"Euro Stoxx 50","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"5,894.46 on 25 Nov","read":"The Euro Stoxx 50 rose 2.04% to 6,014.74 on 26 Nov (ad-hoc-news via onvista; Trading Economics 6,025, +2.2%), recovering most of the previous two sessions’ losses from the May low of 5,894.46 as the tariff cap lifted exporters and chips (ASML +3.2%, Infineon +4%, Santander +4%, BNP Paribas +3%, SAP +2.3% despite a reported 10,000 job cuts). eToro’s Wienke called it rising risk appetite rather than restored confidence, with AI returns the open question. The index is 1.9% below its October close of 6,131.31 and 5.8% under the 13 Nov record of 6,386.86.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below Oct close","src":"ad-hoc-news via onvista (6,014.74, +120.28, +2.04%); Trading Economics (6,025, +2.2%, movers); netz-trends/dpa-AFX (gut zwei Prozent)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://www.ad-hoc-news.de/boerse/news/marktberichte/euro-stoxx-50-closes-2-04-prozent-higher-as-us-rebound-supports-europe/70559393"},{"change":"+2.08% on 26 Nov (+513.39)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":24490.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":24538.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":25284.26},{"l":"Mar 26","v":22680.04},{"l":"Apr 26","v":24292.38},{"l":"May 26","v":25104.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":24995.81},{"l":"Jul 26","v":25629.24},{"l":"Aug 26","v":26258.11},{"l":"Sep 26","v":25199.19},{"l":"Oct 26","v":25818.21},{"l":"26 Nov","v":25215.52}],"latest":"25,215.52","name":"DAX","period":"26 Nov Xetra close","prior":"24,702.13 on 25 Nov","read":"The DAX rose 2.08% to 25,215.52 at the Xetra close on 26 Nov (ad-hoc-news, aktiencheck/comdirect, netz-trends/dpa-AFX), opening at 25,153.11 and peaking at 25,300.88, back above 25,000 after Wednesday’s lowest close since July. 39 of 40 members rose: VW +4.6% and BMW +4.5% on the EU hybrid-import cap from 1 Dec and a JP Morgan outlook, Bayer +4.2% on a Berenberg upgrade and a Hansoh licensing deal, Rheinmetall +4.1%, Siemens Energy +4.0%, Infineon +3.6%, Daimler Truck +3.2%, SAP +2.3% after Bloomberg reported at least 10,000 job cuts; Commerzbank −2.6% was the only loser after UniCredit demanded an EGM to replace nine of ten shareholder board members with Orcel as chair. The MDAX rose 2.34% to 29,263.97. GfK rose to −30.2 the same morning. The index is 2.3% below its October close of 25,818.21 and 6.6% below its 27,001.67 record.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below Oct close","src":"ad-hoc-news (25,215.52, +2.08%); aktiencheck/comdirect (Xetra closes, movers, MDAX); netz-trends/dpa-AFX (+513.39); diebewertung (session context)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Back above 25,000","url":"https://www.ad-hoc-news.de/boerse/news/marktberichte/dax-schliesst-2-08-prozent-hoeher-tech-erholung-stuetzt/70559424"},{"change":"+1.75% on 26 Nov (+182.36; TE news item 10,617, +1.82%)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":9931.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":10223.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":10910.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":10176.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":10378.8},{"l":"May 26","v":10409.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10497.1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":10868.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":10824.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":10606},{"l":"Oct 26","v":10729.78},{"l":"26 Nov","v":10609.3}],"latest":"10,609.30","name":"FTSE 100","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"10,426.94 on 25 Nov","read":"The FTSE 100 rose 1.75% to 10,609.30 on 26 Nov (Trading Economics table and Squawk cash close; the Trading Economics news item says 10,617, +190 points, +1.82%, and Reuters 1.8%), with nearly every constituent higher: Imperial Brands +14% (Reuters 13.3%, highest since June 2017) as Altria weighed an all-cash offer and Japan Tobacco studied a bid, Babcock +6.4% on a five-year £8.7B Ministry of Defence contract, Fresnillo +6.1% and Endeavour over 6% with gold near $3,570, Anglo American, Glencore and Antofagasta over 4%, Rolls-Royce +3.5%, BAE +3.1%, Lloyds +2.8%, Barclays +2.7%, HSBC +2.3%; Unilever and BAT slipped as defensives were sold. The BoE’s Financial Stability Report held the CCyB at 2% and proposed a 50 bp cut in major banks’ Tier 1 requirements. The index is 2.7% below its 13 Nov record of 10,902.56 and 1.1% below its October close; Bank Rate is 4.00% with the next decision on 17 Dec.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below Oct close","src":"Trading Economics (10,609.30, +182.36, +1.75%; news item 10,617, +1.82%; movers); Squawk (cash close +1.75%); Reuters via Yahoo Finance (+1.8%, Imperial Brands, Babcock); BoE FSR (CCyB 2%)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Higher","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-kingdom/stock-market"},{"change":"+0.40 cents at the ECB fixing (TE 1.0961 early 27 Nov)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1747},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.1855},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.1803},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.146},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.1685},{"l":"May 26","v":1.166},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.1422},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.1524},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.1589},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.1341},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.1184},{"l":"26 Nov","v":1.0975}],"latest":"1.0975","name":"EUR/USD","period":"26 Nov (ECB reference)","prior":"1.0944 on 25 Nov (ECB reference 1.0935)","read":"EUR/USD rose to 1.0975 at the ECB reference rate on 26 Nov from 1.0935, trading 1.0957 to 1.0989 (Newsquawk) with the dollar index flat at 103.6 to 103.8 in Thanksgiving trade; Trading Economics shows 1.0961 early on 27 Nov, −0.07%, which implies a 1.0969 close, and its news says the euro edged towards $1.10 on the hawkish ECB account and the de Cos nomination with a 25 bp hike priced for 17 Dec. The pair is 1.9% below its October close of 1.1184 and 0.7% above the 23 Nov low of 1.0903, its weakest since March 2025; the Fed is 97.8% priced for 9 Dec, so the rate differential still caps the euro. A weaker euro adds to imported energy inflation.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Under 1.10","src":"ECB reference rate (1.0975 on 26 Nov; 1.0935 on 25 Nov); Newsquawk (range 1.0957–1.0989, DXY 103.62–103.84); Trading Economics (1.0961 on 27 Nov, −0.07%); JournalArta (1.0975 intraday)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Firmer","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/euro_reference_exchange_rates/html/eurofxref-graph-usd.en.html"},{"change":"+0.80% on 26 Nov (+0.69)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":31.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":28.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":28.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":39.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":46},{"l":"May 26","v":46},{"l":"Jun 26","v":43.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":59.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":69.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":72.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":103.94},{"l":"26 Nov","v":87.75}],"latest":"€87.75","name":"TTF Natural Gas","period":"26 Nov (TE close)","prior":"€87.06 on 25 Nov (TE previous; row showed €87.33)","read":"TTF front-month rose 0.80% to €87.75 per MWh on 26 Nov (Trading Economics; previous 87.06, which differs from the 87.33 recorded for 25 Nov) as forecasts for northwest and central Europe turned colder into early December after Wednesday’s drop on higher Norwegian supply; Newsquawk cited rising TTF on colder December forecasts. Storage is under 68% against a five-year seasonal average of 86% (Trading Economics; GIE 68.9% on 23 Nov against 89% per Oilprice), and Gulf LNG remains disrupted with Iran saying two tankers hit mines near Khasab on the Hormuz crossing and the IRGC claiming control of both sides of the strait. Brent settled $96.57, +1.2%, a third gain, and OPEC+ meets 29 Nov with a rollover expected. No exchange settlement page was opened. The price is about €8 above the €80 bad line and 15.6% below its October close of €103.94.","ref":50,"refLabel":"€50 = warn, €80 = bad","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above €80 bad line","src":"Trading Economics (87.75, +0.69, +0.80%, previous 87.06, storage under 68%); Newsquawk (colder forecasts); Reuters via Investing.com (Brent $96.57, mines near Khasab); Oilprice (GIE 68.9% on 23 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Higher","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"},{"change":"n/a","hist":[],"latest":"≈300 bp","name":"iTraxx Crossover","period":"28 Sep 2026, intraday","prior":"About 315–320 bp in mid-May 2026","read":"No dated level was found for 17 to 26 Nov. The last confirmed print is about 300 bp touched intraday on 28 Sept 2026 (Reuters, S&P Global data), below the 315 to 320 bp range of mid-May. The row stays n/a until a dated page gives a level. The 26 Nov rebound (STOXX 600 +1.8%, all sectors up, BTP spread 107 bp, OAT-Bund about 147 bp) argues for a tighter print than on 25 Nov, while the VIX at 28.29 and the bank-specific news (Commerzbank, BAWAG) say credit is not yet calm.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Stale, 28 Sep","src":"Reuters via Gate (28 Sep 2026); cbonds","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.gate.com/en-us/news/detail/itraxx-european-crossover-index-touches-300-basis-points-for-first-time-17884358"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the core. Closing levels for Thursday 19 Nov unless a date is shown.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["A relief day inside the Risk-off. The STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov (Reuters via Yahoo Finance and Longbridge), its second gain in nine sessions, after USTR said on 25 Nov it intends to cap the new Section 301 tariffs at the rates agreed in trade deals, 15% including MFN duties for the EU, Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Switzerland, not stacked on the 10% and 12.5% forced-labour duties or sectoral tariffs, with nothing taking effect before the 25 Jan hearing. All sectors rose: miners, autos and defence more than 3%, technology after two days of selling (Soitec +7%, Infineon +3.6%, STMicro +4.8%, ASML +3.2%), Imperial Brands +13.3% as Altria weighed an all-cash offer, Babcock +6.6% on an £8.7B submarine contract, Siemens Energy +4.8% on a Venezuela alliance. Commerzbank fell 2.6% after UniCredit called for an EGM to replace nine of ten shareholder board members with Orcel as chair, and BAWAG 5.7% after the Irish High Court blocked its Permanent TSB deal. The Euro Stoxx 50 rose 2.04% to 6,014.74, the DAX 2.08% to 25,215.52 (VW +4.6%, BMW +4.5%, Bayer +4.2%, Rheinmetall +4.1%; SAP +2.3% on a reported 10,000 job cuts), the CAC 40 1.6%, the FTSE MIB 2.4%, Spain over 2% and the FTSE 100 1.75% to 10,609.30 (Trading Economics; its news item says 10,617, +1.82%). US markets were shut; the VIX stands at 28.29 from 25 Nov.","The ECB got a successor and a hawkish account. Eurozone finance ministers agreed and European Council president Costa announced on 26 Nov the nomination of Pablo Hernández de Cos, 55, BIS general manager and Bank of Spain governor 2018 to 2024, to replace Lagarde when she leaves on 7 Feb 2027, after the Dutch dropped Klaas Knot; France’s Laurence Boone and Germany’s Markus Brunnermeier were recommended for the board seats of Schnabel (leaving 3 Jan 2027) and Lane (31 May 2027), with Parliament and Governing Council consultations before a formal appointment in December. Reuters said investors read the choice as policy continuity. The account of the 28 to 29 Oct meeting said a large number of members could have supported a hike then, that it would simply frontload the necessary tightening, that inflation is likely to go higher than earlier thought with fixings peaking at 4.5% in January 2027 and above 2.5% until Q1 2028, and that rates still lie within the range of neutral estimates; futures price a 90% December hike (Investing.com).","German consumers are flat on the floor. GfK consumer climate for December rose to −30.2 from a revised −30.9 (first print −30.8), the highest since July but short of the −29.0 to −29.9 consensus: income expectations −13.9 from −15.7, willingness to buy −11.5 from −11.9, willingness to save 15.6 from 17.2, economic expectations −1.6 from 2.1, the first fall in seven months, and price expectations 2.8 from 1.0; NIM’s Bürkl said the climate is stagnating at a low level and that this does not bode well for Christmas. Euro area M3 grew 3.6% y/y in October (3.5% expected), loans to households 3.1% and to companies 4.5% from 4.2%. French producer prices rose 8.2% y/y in October from 6.9%. The BoE’s Financial Stability Report held the CCyB at 2%, proposed cutting major banks’ Tier 1 requirements by about 50 bp of risk-weighted assets towards a 13% benchmark, noted the gilt up 40 bp since July and Brent at $92, and flagged AI valuations and private credit defaults of 6.3%.","Bonds gave back a little, the periphery tightened and the euro firmed. The Bund rose to 3.26% (Trading Economics 3.2576, +3.9 bp; ANSA 3.25%) as equities rebounded and crude rose, the BTP fell 2.6 bp to 4.32% and the spread closed 107 bp from 111.9 bp after opening at 109 bp (ANSA), and the OAT eased to 4.72% for an OAT-Bund spread of about 147 bp, inside the 150 bp warn line for the first time this month. EUR/USD rose to 1.0975 at the ECB reference rate from 1.0935, trading 1.0957 to 1.0989 (Newsquawk) with the dollar index flat near 103.7 in holiday trade; Trading Economics shows 1.0961 early on 27 Nov. Italy sold €8.5B of 6-month BOTs on 26 Nov (results not confirmed on a page read) and sells medium and long BTPs on 27 Nov; the French Senate votes the revenue part of the budget on 28 Nov.","Energy tightened again. Brent settled $96.57, +$1.96 or 1.2% (Reuters), a third gain and about 5% on the week, after Iran’s Revolutionary Guards said two tankers, one a supertanker with more than 2 million barrels, hit sea mines north of Khasab while crossing Hormuz, the IRGC claimed full control of both sides of the strait, the Houthis claimed the Ras Tanura refinery attack and a drone hit the Slavyansk refinery in Krasnodar; WTI had no settlement for the holiday and was $84.84 at 13:21 ET. OPEC+ meets 29 Nov and is expected to roll over output targets for at least Q1 2027. TTF rose 0.8% to €87.75 (Trading Economics; previous 87.06) on colder December forecasts, with storage under 68% against a five-year average of 86%, still above the €80 bad line. Spain votes 29 Nov with PP plus Vox at 187 seats in the final 40dB poll and 186 in the poll.graphics average (68% of simulations reach 176); Asia was firm on 26 Nov (Nikkei +0.86% to 57,499.16) and the Bank of Korea hiked to 3.25%."],"translation":[{"body":"The STOXX 600 rose 1.8% to 636.98 on 26 Nov, back above the 635 trigger but 3 points short of the 640 restore line, with the VIX at 28.29 from 25 Nov and no US session, so the framework keeps the posture Risk-off until both restore conditions hold at a close. The bounce was broad, every sector up and cyclicals leading (miners, autos, defence over 3%, chips rebounding), which is what a tariff-cap relief rally should look like; it is not yet a trend, as eToro noted, and the two losers were bank-specific (Commerzbank on UniCredit, BAWAG on a blocked deal). The Euro Stoxx 50 at 6,014.74 recovered most of two days’ losses but is still 1.9% under its October close; a STOXX 600 close above 640 on 27 Nov, with US stocks reopening for a half session, would meet the first restore condition, and a close back under 635 would reset the count.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"Core bonds gave back a little of the rally: the Bund rose 3.9 bp to 3.26% as risk appetite returned and Brent rose, while the ECB account showed a large number of members ready to hike in October and futures price a 90% December move. The periphery tightened with the equity bounce and the de Cos nomination: the BTP-Bund spread closed 107 bp from 111.9 bp, well under 150 bp, and the OAT-Bund spread eased to about 147 bp, back inside the warn line ahead of the Senate’s 28 Nov revenue vote. EUR/USD rose to 1.0975 at the fixing with the dollar flat in holiday trade, so the hawkish account and the succession helped the euro at the margin while the Fed at 97.8% for 9 Dec still caps it under 1.10. Italy’s medium and long BTP auction on 27 Nov and Spain’s vote on 29 Nov are the next tests of the calm.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"Policy is tightening into soft data with the leadership now settled: the account says an October hike would merely have frontloaded tightening and that inflation will go higher than thought, a December hike to 2.75% is 90% priced, and Hernández de Cos, read by investors as continuity, takes over on 7 Feb with Boone and Brunnermeier replacing Schnabel and Lane. The reserve-ratio increase is deferred past December, M3 growth is 3.6% and bank lending to firms 4.5%, so liquidity is tightening by price rather than quantity. TTF at €87.75 and Brent at $96.57 after the mine strikes keep the energy channel to the 1 Dec flash HICP open; the BoE eased bank capital by about 50 bp while holding the CCyB at 2% and Bank Rate at 4.00%. Liquidity for European risk improves only if the VIX falls under 25 and TTF back under €80; neither holds now.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
